            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest news is that NASA’s Perseverance rover has captured a first-of-its-kind view from Mars: Earth disappearing behind the Martian moon Phobos. NASA/JPL says the Mastcam-Z image sequence was taken on July 2, 2026 — sol 1,907 of the mission — and shows Earth as a tiny bright dot being occulted by Phobos in the Martian evening sky. NASA described it as the first time humanity has captured Earth slipping behind another object from the surface of another planet【5:0†source】.\n\nOther recent Perseverance updates include:\n\n- **A major driving milestone:** Perseverance completed the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars — 26.2 miles / 42.195 km — reaching that distance on sol 1,890 after about five years and four months of driving【5:4†source】.\n- **Exploration of ancient rocks near Jezero Crater’s rim:** NASA reported in July that Perseverance found evidence that a roughly 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of rocks at the “Broom Point member” was built by repeated ancient asteroid impacts【5:2†source】.\n- **Long-term mission health:** NASA/JPL reported that the rover remains in excellent shape, with tests indicating its systems can support operations until at least 2031. The rover has been heading toward a region nicknamed “Lac de Charmes,” where the team plans more rock sampling【5:1†source】.",
